<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Books and Movies – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> My Favorite Movie Is... Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a brief overview of a favorite book or movie. Interactive: Participate in class discussions, sharing reviews of various types of content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a brief overview of a favorite movie in class discussions (e.g., "My favorite movie is about space exploration").
                Can participate in class discussions by asking and answering questions about favorite books (e.g., "What book do you recommend?" "I recommend this book because it's exciting").
                Can share opinions in group discussions about favorite books (e.g., "I recommend reading this book because it is about friendship and adventure").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of various movie posters and book covers (e.g., action, comedy, fantasy, documentary). Students briefly discuss with a partner which types they recognize and if they prefer movies or books in general. The teacher asks open-ended questions like, "What genre do you see here? Which looks more engaging?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express preferences and give reasons using the target grammar and vocabulary. For example, the teacher says, "My favorite movie is *The Jungle Book* because it's an engaging adventure story with amazing characters." (Pronunciation focus: practicing /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ in verbs like "likes," "watches," "enjoys"). The teacher also models "I prefer watching documentaries over comedy movies because they are more educating and I learn new things." (Pronunciation focus: contrastive vowel sounds /ʊ/ in "full" and /uː/ in "fool" through examples of similar words).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'My favorite movie is *Spider-Man*', does it mean I like it a little or a lot?" (Expected answer: "A lot.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I prefer reading fiction books over poetry,' which one do I like more?" (Expected answer: "Fiction books.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies"</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (or teacher-read dialogue) between two teenagers, Maria and Juan, discussing their favorite movie genres and recent books they've enjoyed. The dialogue uses present simple for preferences, past simple for recent watches/reads, `verb + -ing + noun + because`, and comparative preferences (e.g., "I like reading fiction books because they are exciting." "I watched a comedy yesterday. It was about a funny character." "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling."). Key vocabulary like `character, plot, genre, entertaining, engaging, emotional` is included.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand main ideas and specific details when listening to short dialogues about preferences and opinions regarding books and movies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Maria and Juan talking about?" (Expected answer: Their favorite books and movies.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and answer True or False questions based on the dialogue. E.g., "Maria prefers action movies." "Juan read a mystery book last week."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match the movie/book mentioned to its genre or a brief description from the dialogue.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using present simple for preferences and reasons (e.g., "I like reading because it's relaxing."), verb + -ing + noun + because, past simple for recently watched/read content, and comparing preferences (e.g., "I prefer documentaries over fiction because they are more engaging."). Vocabulary includes `movie, book, character, plot, genre, describe, like, prefer, entertaining, engaging, emotional, favorite`. Pronunciation focus on `/s/, /z/, /ɪz/` in third person verbs and `/ʊ/, /uː/` contrast. Pragmatic function: giving opinions and comparing preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion / Gap-Fill (Individual/Choral):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks focusing on the target grammar and vocabulary. Students complete them orally. E.g., "I like _______ (watch) adventure movies because they are very ______ (excite)." "My sister _______ (enjoy) _______ (read) fantasy books because they _______ (have) amazing ______ (plot)." The teacher guides choral repetition and individual responses, correcting pronunciation of verbs like "watches," "reads," "enjoys."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Preference Substitution Drill (Pairs):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames and prompts for substitution. E.g., "I prefer [GENRE A] movies over [GENRE B] movies because they are more [ADJECTIVE]." Pairs take turns substituting different genres (`comedy, action, documentary, drama`) and adjectives (`thrilling, funny, emotional, relaxing`). The teacher circulates, providing feedback on grammar and natural intonation for comparing preferences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Ask Your Partner" (Pairs):</b> Students interview each other using guided questions on a handout. Questions include: "What's your favorite movie genre and why?" "What kind of books do you prefer reading?" "Did you watch any interesting movie last weekend? What was it about?" "Do you prefer books or movies, and why?" Students note down their partner's answers, practicing active listening and asking follow-up questions. They also provide constructive feedback to their partners on their responses.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Books and Movies"</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Movie/Book Review Panel" - Small group discussion and short peer presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each student has 1-2 minutes to give a brief overview of their favorite movie or book. They must include the title, genre, a main character or part of the plot, and explain why it is their favorite using at least one reason (e.g., "My favorite movie is [title] because it's [adjective] and the plot is [adjective]."). They use sequencers like "First, I want to talk about..." or "Finally, I recommend this because...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each student shares, the group engages in a brief discussion (2-3 minutes per student). Group members ask questions like, "What book do you recommend?" or "Why do you prefer that genre?" They also share their own opinions and compare preferences (e.g., "I think that movie was very funny." "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to provide constructive feedback to their peers after each presentation or discussion round.</li>
                </ol>
                This activity promotes fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, directly addressing the theme "My Favorite Movie Is... Because..." and scenario "Books and Movies."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' participation and oral presentations during the "Movie/Book Review Panel" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, especially of target sounds (/s/, /z/, /ɪz/, /ʊ/, /uː/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Effectively using words like `genre, plot, character, entertaining, engaging, emotional`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Ability to give a brief, understandable overview and express opinions with reasons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct use of present simple for preferences/reasons, verb + -ing + noun + because, past simple for recent content, and comparative forms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Asking and answering questions, providing constructive feedback, and participating actively in group discussions.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned today about expressing opinions on books and movies is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I used successfully today is..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about talking about my favorite movies or books and explaining why I like them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of speaking in English today was most challenging for me, and why?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The teacher reflects on the lesson's effectiveness.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of the speaking activities worked particularly well to encourage student interaction and use of target language? Were the students engaged emotionally and creatively?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grammatical structures or vocabulary items need further reinforcement in future lessons? Were the pronunciation points clear for students? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support students in writing their reviews?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The speaking practice from this lesson (Lesson 3) directly prepares students for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), where they will construct written reviews or summaries of their favorite books or movies, leveraging the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced orally today. Students will be able to organize their thoughts, using sequencers like `first, next, after, finally` and adverbs `creatively, emotionally` to describe their preferences in writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>